---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import Chevron from '../components/Chevron.astro';
import { REPO, REPO_LABEL } from '../data/site.mjs';

/**
 * Three stages, in order, each producing the foundation for the next: explore
 * and collect, organise into a system, build from the system. Tyler's copy.
 *
 * Everything here is a pattern that already exists — the lede, the section
 * headings, the prose lists and their marks, the artifact link with its
 * chevron. No new component was introduced to carry it.
 *
 * The colour-pair table, the requirements ledger and the proof cells the
 * previous version ended on went with that version. `src/data/rubric.mjs` was
 * their only consumer and is now unreferenced.
 */

/** What a working option can surface that a specification cannot. */
const questions = [
  'Do two people understand the same requirement differently?',
  'Does the proposed hierarchy survive real content?',
  'What happens outside the default state?',
  'Does the interaction solve the intended problem?',
  'Which assumptions have not been discussed?',
];

/** What I read the built result against. */
const review = [
  'Does the behavior support the intended outcome?',
  'Do the states, permissions, and failure paths make sense?',
  'Does the work strengthen the product’s patterns?',
  'Does the content explain what happened and what comes next?',
  'Can people perceive and operate it in real conditions?',
  'Is the behavior credible in the production environment?',
];

/** The loop, stated once at the end. */
const compound = [
  'Exploration produces better evidence.',
  'The system turns that evidence into reusable decisions.',
  'Implementation applies those decisions to working software.',
];
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="I explore broadly, turn what I learn into a system, and build from it"
  metaTitle="How I design with AI | Tyler Young"
  description="AI can turn an idea into working software in minutes. It cannot make the idea sound. I use it in three stages: explore the problem and collect evidence, organise what we learn into a system, then build from that system."
  descriptionDraft
  eyebrow="How I design with AI"
  current="building"
  meta={[
    ['Practice', 'AI-assisted product design'],
    ['My role', 'Explore · systematize · build'],
    ['Output', 'More informed options · clearer decisions · working software'],
    [
      'Principle',
      'AI expands what I can produce. I remain responsible for what it means and what ships.',
    ],
  ]}
  links={[
    { label: 'The design system', href: '/design-system', external: true },
    { label: REPO_LABEL, href: REPO },
  ]}
>
  <p class="lede">
    AI can turn an idea into working software in minutes. That makes execution
    faster, but it does not make the idea sound.
  </p>
  <p>
    The quality of the result still depends on what the team understands before
    building: the problem, the people affected, the behavior required, and the
    decisions that should remain consistent across the product.
  </p>
  <p>
    I use AI in three stages. First, I explore the problem and collect evidence.
    Then I organize what we learn into a system. Finally, I build from that
    system.
  </p>
  <p>Each stage produces the foundation for the next.</p>

  <h2>1. Explore and collect</h2>
  <p>I begin by expanding the team’s understanding of the problem.</p>
  <p>
    AI helps me examine source material, compare approaches, test assumptions,
    and turn early ideas into working options. Because those options are
    inexpensive to produce, the team can explore more of the problem before
    committing to one answer.
  </p>
  <p>The goal at this stage is not volume for its own sake. It is evidence.</p>
  <p>
    A working option can reveal questions that a specification or static screen
    leaves hidden:
  </p>

  <ul>
    {questions.map((q) => <li>{q}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p>
    I define the inquiry before generating solutions. The brief identifies the
    user’s goal, the required behavior, the known constraints, the open
    questions, and the conditions that would make a result wrong.
  </p>
  <p>
    That framing gives the exploration a purpose. Without it, AI can produce
    many polished answers to an unresolved question.
  </p>
  <p>
    Running software makes the findings more concrete. A team can use an option,
    encounter its behavior, and respond to what it does — not what they imagine
    it might do.
  </p>
  <p>
    In one stakeholder review, I published a working application and received 48
    notes across six product surfaces. The artifact made the feedback specific.
    It also revealed where the reviewer’s wording, the visible interface, and
    the right product response did not align.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a class="artifact" href="/review-loop">See the stakeholder review<Chevron /></a>
  </p>
  <p>
    Exploration ends when the team has learned enough to make decisions. Those
    decisions become the system.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Organize what we learned into a system</h2>
  <p>
    Individual findings do not create a coherent product. They must be turned
    into rules that can survive beyond the screen where they first appeared.
  </p>
  <p>
    I organize the decisions from exploration into a system: principles,
    behaviors, components, content patterns, accessibility requirements, and
    visual relationships.
  </p>
  <p>
    The system records what should remain consistent and what may vary. It turns
    a resolved question into a reusable constraint.
  </p>
  <p>
    For example, if several operational states must remain distinguishable in
    dense interfaces, the solution should not be recreated card by card. The
    system should define how each state communicates through language, shape,
    and color. Every later component can then inherit that decision.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is why tokens and components matter. They are not the story; they are
    how a decision becomes enforceable.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the system contains an approved pattern, AI can apply it. If no pattern
    exists, the missing value remains visible as a design question. The agent
    should not hide that question by inventing a local answer.
  </p>
  <p>
    At Atheer, I used this approach to rebuild an aging design system. Product
    decisions became working components with documented states. Designers could
    explore without breaking coherence. Engineers received behavior in code.
    Brand and accessibility rules applied during implementation instead of being
    repaired afterward.
  </p>
  <p>
    The running product and its published component library show both sides of
    that system.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a class="artifact" href="/ops-manager">See the operations product and component library<Chevron /></a>
  </p>
  <p>
    A useful system does more than make a product consistent. It carries what
    the team learned into the next implementation.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Build from the system</h2>
  <p>
    Once the intent is clear and the decisions are encoded, AI can build without
    redefining the product along the way.
  </p>
  <p>
    The agent works from the brief and the system. It can assemble approved
    components, apply established patterns, implement required states, and run
    defined checks. Because those decisions already exist, implementation
    becomes faster without becoming arbitrary.
  </p>
  <p>The resulting software then returns to people for review.</p>
  <p>I evaluate it against the problem we agreed to solve:</p>

  <ul>
    {review.map((q) => <li>{q}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p>
    A result can pass its technical checks and still be the wrong thing to ship.
    AI can confirm that an implementation follows the instructions. It cannot
    independently confirm that the instructions reflect the right product
    decision.
  </p>
  <p>
    That boundary matters most when the requirements are ambiguous. An agent can
    implement an assumption with the same confidence and speed as an approved
    decision. I therefore use AI to accelerate execution, not to conceal
    unresolved judgment.
  </p>
  <p>
    In Alucify, I designed product surfaces that make this boundary visible:
    what the system knows, what it inferred, what a change could affect, and
    where a person still needs to decide.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a class="artifact" href="/alucify">See the Alucify case study<Chevron /></a>
  </p>
  <p>
    The build is not the end of the process. Once people use it, the product
    produces new evidence. That evidence begins another round of exploration.
  </p>

  <h2>The three stages compound</h2>
  <p>The value of AI does not come from generating a screen faster.</p>
  <p>It comes from connecting three kinds of work:</p>

  <ol>
    {compound.map((c) => <li>{c}</li>)}
  </ol>

  <p>
    The built product then reveals new information, and the process begins again
    with a stronger foundation.
  </p>
  <p>
    This changes where design has leverage. I spend less time reproducing
    settled decisions and more time investigating the problem, making those
    decisions explicit, and judging their results.
  </p>
  <p>
    AI increases the range and speed of the work. The system preserves what the
    team has learned. I remain responsible for the meaning and quality of what
    ships.
  </p>
</DocumentLayout>
